      "description": "Watch Walsall vs Leyton Orient highlights and goals from Carabao on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on August 25, 2026. The Carabao Cup clash between Walsall and Leyton Orient on August 25, 2026, proved to be a grueling test of nerves and defensive discipline. With a place in the next round at stake, neither side was willing to give an inch, leading to a tactical battle that remained deadlocked through the duration of normal time. A First Half of Thwarted Ambitions From the opening whistle, both teams displayed intent but found themselves frustrated by excellent goalkeeping. Walsall looked dangerous early on, with Reece Smith forcing an impressive save from Noah Phillips in the 13th minute. Leyton Orient responded with pressure of their own as Demetri Mitchell and Charlie Wellens tested Jed Ward , who remained equal to everything thrown his way. Despite the creative efforts of Albert Adomah and several half-chances for Tai Sodje , the defenses held firm, leaving the scoreline blank as the teams headed into the break. Momentum Shifts and Defensive Resilience The second half saw an increase in physical intensity, characterized by several bookings and forced tactical adjustments. Isaac Hayden for the visitors and Tai Sodje for the hosts both received yellow cards as the midfield battle intensified. Injuries also played a role, with Walsall's James Furlong and Leyton Orient's Will Forrester being forced off the pitch. As the clock ticked down, the visitors mounted a late charge; Jaze Kabia saw a golden opportunity saved by Jed Ward in the 88th minute, while Joseph Olowu headed wide in stoppage time, ensuring the match would be decided from the penalty spot. Shootout Drama Decides the Tie The ensuing penalty shootout was a rollercoaster of emotions. Walsall faced an early setback when Lewis Simper's effort was saved, but Leyton Orient failed to capitalize immediately as Jaze Kabia missed his attempt. A string of successful conversions followed, with Courtney Clarke , Mason Hancock , and Tai Sodje all finding the net for the hosts. The tension reached its peak in the sudden-death rounds until Declan Skura’s shot was saved by the hero of the night, Noah Phillips . Joseph Olowu then stepped up and calmly slotted his strike into the bottom left corner, clinching a 6-5 shootout victory for Leyton Orient ."
